  "query_preview": "..\n\nPrevailing northwest flow in the mid-upper levels will persist \nacross the Great lakes region for the next couple days given the \nposition of the large mid level ridge extending across the Rockies \nand high plains. \n\nAn ongoing gradual influx of high low level theta e air will \ncontribute to a warmer and more humid day today. This in turn will \ntranslate to building instability during the course of the day, with \nvarious hi res solutions forecasting 0-1km MLCAPE values ranging \nfrom 1k to 2k J/kg. The northwest flow will also contribute to a \nstrongly sheared environment, with 0-6km bulk shear values forecast \nto range from 30 to 50 knots across Se Mi. Model soundings indicate \na mid level warm layer resulting in a respectable degree of capping, \nwhich will likely limit convective coverage today. Subtle large \nscale forcing via low a amplitude mid level short wave will offer a \npotential for some erosion of the cap today, warranting low chance \npops. Taking into consideration the deep layer shear and good \ndowndraft CAPE, any thunderstorm that initiates will be capable of \nproducing damaging wind gusts. \n\nRecent CAMs solutions continue to indicate the potential for several \nclusters of thunderstorms across the Great Lakes region tonight \nthrough Monday night. Trends within the model solution space are \nsuggesting the higher convective probabilities tonight into the \nfirst half of the day Monday residing either over western Lower Mi \nor Wisconsin/Illinois along an axis of high instability. Within the \nmodel probability space, convective potential across Se Mi increases \nlate Monday afternoon into Monday night. A stronger mid level short \nwave and associated sfc front will provide the dynamic forcing to \ntrigger more widespread convection. A continued strongly sheared \nenvironment will remain supportive of severe convection, the coverage\nand intensity of which will rely on available instability. The \nuncertainty in early day convective trends will play heavily into \nlate day available instability. The potential afternoon recovery in \ntemps with sfc dewpoints in the upper 60s/near 70 suggest diurnal \nrecovery may be swift. For this reason, SPC continues to have all of \nSe Mi in a slight risk Monday. The uncertainty in the evolution of \nearly day thunderstorm clusters has prevented an outlook upgrade at \nthis time. \n\nAmplification of the longer wavelength trough across the mid \nAtlantic/northeast toward mid week will drive slight cooler and \ndrier air across Lower Mi. Increasing anticyclonic flow will also \nwarrant an overall dry forecast mid week.",
